 - Bash: git blame -w -M -C --line-porcelain lib/index.js | grep '^author ' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -8; wc -l lib/index.js; git log -3 --format='%an %ad' --date=short -- lib; git log --format='%an %ad' --date=short --author="Troy Goode" -1 -- lib
FINAL ANSWER:
**Bus factor of `lib/`: 1.**

`lib/` holds one file, `lib/index.js` (238 lines). The numbers below come from git history.

| Measure | Result |
|---|---|
| Lines still attributed to Troy Goode (`git blame`) | 138 of 238 (58%) |
| Commits touching `lib/` by Troy Goode | 33 of 66 (50%) |
| Next biggest contributors by blame | Douglas Christopher Wilson 18 lines, Alejandro Caravaca Puchades 12, Raymond Feng 7, LuisEGR 6 |
| Distinct commit authors | 25 |

**What it means:**
- If Troy Goode leaves, the person who wrote about half to 58% of the code is gone. Nobody else has more than about 8% of the lines. So 1 departure takes out the main maintainer.
- The count is lower than the author list suggests. 20 of the 25 authors made exactly 1 commit, and most of those are small fixes. Knowledge is spread thin.
- The "Fiona Formatter" lines (32) are from the latest style-only reformat, commit `62628c0`. They are not real ownership. Those lines were most likely written by earlier authors, so Troy's real share is probably higher than 58%.

**Activity:**
- Troy's last commit to `lib/` was 2026-04-10. The last commit of any kind to `lib/` was the reformat on 2026-04-12.
- No one has committed to `lib/` in the last 90 days. Under the skill's 90-day rule, even the primary owner counts as inactive.

**Limits of this analysis:**
- Commit share and blame show who wrote the code. They don't show who understands it. Reviewers and issue discussions aren't counted.
- With a single file, there is no per-module breakdown or importance ranking to report.
- A 238-line file is small enough that a new maintainer could learn it quickly. The practical risk is lower than the number suggests.

**Cheap fix:** have a second person review or touch `lib/index.js` in the next change. Doing that for two or three changes would raise the bus factor to 2.

